      "input_context": {
        "user_input_script": "This is a reimagining of the classic legend, titled TARZAN: GHOST OF THE BURNING SANDS.\n\nThe lush jungles of the Congo are replaced by the vast, unforgiving \"Sea of Glass\"—a desert so brutal that only the most resilient life survives. Tarzan is not a swinger of vines, but a master of the dunes, a \"sand-surfer\" who moves with the fluid grace of a sidewinder.\n\nTARZAN: GHOST OF THE BURNING SANDS\nCHARACTERS:\n\nTARKAN (TARZAN): Mid-20s. Skin bronzed to the color of cedar, eyes a piercing, startling blue. He wears wrappings of sun-bleached hide and carries a spear tipped with obsidian.\n\nJANE PORTER: 20s. An adventurous hydrologist and cartographer. She is resilient but exhausted by the heat.\n\nCLAYTON: 40s. A mercenary hired to find \"The Blue Vein\"—a legendary subterranean river.\n\nKALA: A matriarchal Hamadryas baboon, larger and more predatory than those known to science.\n\nSCENE 1: THE INFINITE ERG - DAY\nThe sun is a white-hot coin in a bleached sky.\n\nA massive sandstorm—a haboob—towers a thousand feet high, swallowing the world. In the chaos of the past, a steam-powered crawler (an 1890s desert tank) lies half-buried, its iron hull sandblasted to a shine.\n\nNARRATOR (V.O.)\nThe desert does not forgive. It does not forget. It only consumes.\n\nA young boy crawls from the wreckage of the crawler. He is alone. A shadow looms over him. It is KALA. She is gray-furred, scarred, and ancient. She doesn't snarl; she tilts her head, feeling the heat off the child. She reaches out a leathery hand.\n\nCUT TO:\n\nSCENE 2: THE CANYON OF WHISPERS - FIFTEEN YEARS LATER\nThe silence of the desert is broken by a rhythmic shick-shick-shick.\n\nTARKAN (Tarzan) is \"surfing\" down a 300-foot dune. He uses a flat piece of polished chitin (from a giant desert beetle) as a board. He moves at 40 miles per hour, trailing a plume of golden dust.\n\nHe reaches the bottom, leaps, and digs his fingers into a rock face. He climbs with the speed of a lizard.\n\nHe reaches a high ledge where a troop of GIANT BABOONS waits. They don't hoot; they communicate in low-frequency clicks and whistles that mimic the wind. Tarkan mimics them perfectly. He drops a freshly caught desert hare at Kala’s feet. She grunts—a sign of respect.\n\nSCENE 3: THE EXPEDITION CAMP - NIGHT\nA small oasis, barely a puddle. Three tents are pitched.\n\nJANE PORTER sits by a flickering oil lamp, sketching a map. CLAYTON stands at the edge of the light, cleaning a long-range rifle.\n\nJANE\nThe water table is dropping, Clayton. If we don’t find the source within forty-eight hours, the camels won't make the return trip.\n\nCLAYTON\nThe \"Blue Vein\" exists, Jane. My sensors picked up the seismic resonance. There’s an ocean under these sands. And where there’s water in a wasteland, there’s a fortune.\n\nJANE\n(Disgusted)\nYou’re looking for a payday. I’m looking for the reason this entire ecosystem hasn't collapsed.\n\nA shadow flits across the top of a nearby dune. Clayton snaps his rifle up.\n\nCLAYTON\nMovement. Out there.\n\nJANE\n(Standing)\nWait! Don’t shoot. It might be one of the nomads.\n\nCLAYTON\nToo fast for a nomad.\n\nSCENE 4: THE AMBUSH - DAY\nJane is scouting a narrow canyon. She’s separated from the group, following a series of strange markings on the stone—petroglyphs that look fresh.\n\nSuddenly, the ground erupts. A DUNE-RIPPER (a prehistoric, land-dwelling crocodilian) bursts from the sand. It is fifteen feet of muscle and scales the color of dust.\n\nJane falls back, her pistol jamming as sand gets into the chamber. The Ripper lunges.\n\nWHOOSH.\n\nA spear thrashes into the Ripper’s neck. TARKAN drops from the canyon wall like a falling star. He lands on the beast’s back. He doesn’t scream a jungle yell; he emits a piercing, high-pitched WHISTLE that echoes off the walls, disorienting the predator.\n\nHe uses a bone knife to finish the job. The beast collapses.\n\nTarkan stands. He is a silhouette against the blinding sun. Jane gazes up, shielding her eyes.\n\nJANE\nYou... you saved me.\n\nTarkan tilts his head. He approaches her with the caution of a wolf. He smells her—scents of lavender soap and sweat. He touches the fabric of her sleeve.\n\nTARKAN\n(In a voice like grinding stones)\nNot... sand.\n\nJANE\n(Breathless)\nNo. It’s cotton. I’m Jane.\n\nTarkan points to himself.\n\nTARKAN\nTar-Kan. (He points to the sun) Child of the Fire.\n\nSCENE 5: THE HIDDEN GARDEN - NIGHT\nTarkan has led Jane to his \"home\"—not a treehouse, but a series of interconnected caverns cooled by a subterranean spring. It is a lush, hidden grotto of ferns and phosphorescent moss.\n\nThey sit by a pool of crystal-clear water.\n\nJANE\nYou’ve been here your whole life, haven't you? You’re the one who’s been protecting the water.\n\nTarkan watches his reflection. He touches the scars on his chest.\n\nTARKAN\nOthers come. With loud sticks. (He mimics a gun blast). They take. The sand grows angry.\n\nJANE\nClayton. He’s going to blast the shelf to get to the aquifer. Tarkan, if he does that, the pressure will blow this entire oasis apart. It’ll dry up forever.\n\nTarkan looks at her. He understands \"dry.\" He understands \"dead.\" He stands, grabbing his spear.\n\nSCENE 6: THE FINAL CONFRONTATION - DAY\nAt the \"Eye of the Desert\"—a massive salt flat—Clayton’s men are setting seismic charges. The machines hum with a low, vibrating growl that agitates the ground.\n\nCLAYTON\nSet the timers! We blow the cap, and we’ll have a geyser a hundred feet high!\n\nSuddenly, the wind picks up. But it’s not the wind. It’s the whistle.\n\nFrom the crest of every surrounding dune, the Sand-Stalkers (the baboons) appear. Hundreds of them. They sit silent, watching.\n\nIn the center, Tarkan appears. He is riding a massive, tamed Dune-Ripper, his feet locked into a saddle of hide.\n\nCLAYTON\nWhat in the hell...\n\nTARKAN\n(Booming)\nLEAVE. THE. WATER.\n\nCLAYTON\nA wild man? You’re the one the legends talk about. The Ghost.\n\nClayton raises his rifle.\n\nCLAYTON\nYou’re just meat and bone, Ghost!\n\nClayton fires. Tarkan leans, the bullet grazing his shoulder. He kicks the Ripper into a gallop.\n\nThe battle is a whirlwind of sand and steel. The baboons descend like a gray tide, dismantling the machinery with brute strength. Tarkan leaps from his mount, tackling Clayton into a deep sand-pit.\n\nThey struggle. The sand is \"quick\"—a literal sinkhole triggered by the vibrations of the machines.\n\nCLAYTON\n(Sinking)\nHelp me! Grab the rope!\n\nTarkan looks at the rope, then at the dying plants at the edge of the oasis. He looks at Jane, who is watching from the ridge.\n\nTarkan throws the rope—not to Clayton, but to the detonator box, yanking it away from the hole just as Clayton sinks to his chest.\n\nTARKAN\nThe desert... decides.\n\nTarkan reaches down and grabs Clayton by the collar, hauling him out with superhuman strength, but tosses him toward his fleeing mercenaries.\n\nTARKAN\nGo. Or stay... and become the sand.\n\nSCENE 7: EPILOGUE - SUNSET\nThe mercenaries are gone. The \"Eye of the Desert\" is quiet.\n\nJane stands by her remaining supplies. A rescue crawler is visible on the horizon.\n\nJANE\nCome with me, Tarkan. There are cities. Rivers that never end. You don't have to fight for every drop of life.\n\nTarkan looks out over the dunes. The sand is turning a deep, royal purple as the stars begin to wake.\n\nTARKAN\nThe desert is harsh. But it is true.\n\nHe takes her hand and presses a small, smooth desert rose (a stone formation) into her palm.\n\nTARKAN\nTell them... the Ghost is watching the water.\n\nHe turns and runs. Not a clumsy human run, but a beautiful, bounding sprint. He hits a steep dune and \"surfs\" down the other side, disappearing into the shadows of the shifting sands.\n\nJane watches until he is gone. She looks at her map, then takes a pen and crosses out the location of the oasis, marking it simply: MIRAGE.\n\nFADE OUT."
      },
